WEBVTT

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:04.590
Gradient Boost, part one.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.150
Regression main ideas.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.830
StatQuest.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.310
Hello, I'm Josh Starmer and welcome to

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.470
StatQuest. Today, we're going to talk

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.110
about the gradient boost machine

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.790
learning algorithm. Specifically, we're

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.950
going to focus on how gradient boost is

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.870
used for regression.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.590
Note, this StatQuest assumes you already

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.830
understand decision trees. So, if you're

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.990
not already down with those, check out

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.950
the quest.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.190
This StatQuest also assumes that you are

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.950
familiar with AdaBoost and the trade-off

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.390
between bias and variance. If not, check

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.230
out the quests. The links are in the

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.790
description below.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.990
This StatQuest is the first part in a

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.070
series that explains how the gradient

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.190
boost machine learning algorithm works.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.470
Specifically, we'll use this data

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.150
where we have the height measurements

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.830
from six people, their favorite colors,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.549
their genders,

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:07.070
and their weights.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.790
And we'll walk through, step-by-step,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.870
the most common way that gradient boost

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.950
fits a model to this training data.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.230
Note, when gradient boost is used to

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.230
predict a continuous value, like weight,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.310
we say that we are using gradient boost

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.870
for regression.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.190
Using gradient boost for regression is

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.350
different from doing linear regression.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.310
So, while the two methods are related,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.870
don't get them confused with each other.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.190
Part two in this series will dive deep

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.150
into the math behind the gradient boost

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.230
algorithm for regression, walking

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.750
through it step-by-step and proving that

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.350
what we covered today is correct.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.590
Part three in this series shows how

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.110
gradient boost can be used for

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.390
classification.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.670
Specifically, we'll walk through,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.990
step-by-step, the most common way

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.230
gradient boost can classify someone as

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.070
either loving the movie Troll 2 or not

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.590
loving Troll 2.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.109
Part four will return to the math behind

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:10.469
gradient boost, this time focusing on

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.550
classification, walking through it

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.750
step-by-step.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.470
Note, the gradient boost algorithm looks

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.630
complicated because it was designed to

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.990
be configured in a wide variety of ways.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.430
But, the reality is that 99% of the time

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.350
only one configuration is used to

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.350
predict continuous values like weight,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.110
and one configuration is used to

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.030
classify samples into different

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.230
categories.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.590
This StatQuest focuses on showing you

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.949
the most common way gradient boost is

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:45.430
used to predict a continuous value like

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.430
weight.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.710
If you are familiar with AdaBoost, then

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.670
a lot of gradient boost will seem very

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.470
similar.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.870
So, let's briefly compare and contrast

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.990
AdaBoost and gradient boost.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.949
If we want to use these measurements to

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:02.750
predict weight,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.229
then AdaBoost starts by building a very

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:07.710
short tree called a stump from the

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.550
training data.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.430
And then the amount of say that the new

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.430
stump has on the final output is based

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.270
on how well it compensated for those

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.270
previous errors.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.630
Then AdaBoost builds the next stump

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.750
based on errors that the previous stump

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.710
made.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.949
In this example, the new stump did a

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:30.310
poor job compensating for the previous

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.870
stump's errors, and its size reflects

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.670
its reduced amount of say.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.390
Then AdaBoost builds another stump based

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.070
on the errors made by the previous

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.870
stump.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.590
And this stump did a little better than

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.350
the last stump, so it's a little larger.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.590
Then AdaBoost continues to make stumps

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.390
in this fashion until it has made the

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.030
number of stumps you asked for or it has

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.470
a perfect fit.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.190
In contrast, gradient boost starts by

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.750
making a single leaf instead of a tree

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.990
or stump.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.470
This leaf represents an initial guess

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.110
for the weights for all of the samples.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.990
When trying to predict a continuous

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.710
value like weight, the first guess is

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.190
the average value.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.550
Then gradient boost builds a tree.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.150
Like AdaBoost, this tree is based on the

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.230
errors made by the previous tree.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.670
But unlike AdaBoost, this tree is

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.550
usually larger than a stump.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.670
That said, gradient boost still

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.430
restricts the size of the tree.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.310
In the simple example that we will go

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.390
through in this stat quest, we will

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.190
build trees with up to four leaves, but

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.310
no larger.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.430
However, in practice, people often set

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:50.310
the maximum number of leaves to be

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.310
between eight and 32.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:56.030
Thus, like AdaBoost, gradient boost

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.230
builds fixed-size trees based on the

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.870
previous trees' errors, but unlike

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.430
AdaBoost, each tree can be larger than a

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.750
stump.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.750
Also, like AdaBoost, gradient boost

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.790
scales the trees. However, gradient

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.070
boost scales all trees by the same

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.030
amount.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.470
Then gradient boost builds another tree

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.550
based on the errors made by the previous

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.230
tree.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.550
And then it scales the tree.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.470
And gradient boost continues to build

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.790
trees in this fashion until it has made

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.070
the number of trees you asked for or

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.230
additional trees fail to improve the

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.350
fit.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.390
Now that we know the main similarities

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.630
and differences between gradient boost

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.510
and AdaBoost,

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.790
let's see how the most common gradient

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.110
boost configuration would use this

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.790
training data to predict weight.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.950
The first thing we do is calculate the

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.910
average weight.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.190
This is the first attempt at predicting

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.670
everyone's weight.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.430
In other words, if we stopped right now,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.550
we would predict that everyone weighed

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.190
71.2 kg.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.670
However, gradient boost doesn't stop

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.590
here.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.990
The next thing we do is build a tree

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.430
based on the errors from the first tree.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.950
The errors that the previous tree made

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.990
are the differences between the observed

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.990
weights and the predicted weight, 71.2.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.430
So, let's start by plugging in 71.2

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.030
for the predicted weight.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.150
And then plug in the first observed

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.110
weight.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:41.110
And do the math.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.230
And save the difference, which is called

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.310
a pseudo residual, in a new column.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:50.590
Note, the term pseudo residual is based

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.310
on linear regression, where the

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.590
difference between the observed values

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.750
and the predicted values results in

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.350
residuals.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.070
The pseudo part of pseudo residual is a

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:03.750
reminder that we are doing gradient

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.430
boost, not linear regression, and is

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.270
something I'll talk more about in part

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.030
two of this series when we go through

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.270
the math.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.030
Now, we do the same thing for the

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.070
remaining weights.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:20.230
Now, we will build a tree using height,

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.950
favorite color, and gender

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.710
to predict the residuals.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.670
If it seems strange to predict the

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.710
residuals instead of the original

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:32.750
weights, just bear with me, and soon all

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.510
will become clear.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.350
So, setting aside the reason why we are

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:40.510
building a tree to predict the residuals

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:44.350
for the time being, here's the tree.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.990
Remember, in this example, we are only

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.670
allowing up to four leaves.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:52.150
But when using a larger data set, it is

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.590
common to allow anywhere from eight to

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.030
32.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:58.870
By restricting the total number of

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.150
leaves, we get fewer leaves than

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.430
residuals.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.350
As a result, these two rows of data go

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.790
to the same leaf.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.470
So, we replace these residuals with

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:13.590
average.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.670
And these two rows of data go to the

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.830
same leaf.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:20.150
So, we replace these residuals with

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.710
their average.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.790
Now, we can combine the original leaf

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.630
with the new tree

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:29.550
to make a new prediction of an

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:31.710
individual's weight from the training

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:33.670
data.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:35.870
We start with the initial prediction,

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.830
71.2.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.870
Then, we run the data down the tree,

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:45.470
and we get 16.8.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.430
So, the predicted weight equals 71.2

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.510
+ 16.8,

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:54.230
which equals 88.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.110
Which is the same as the observed

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:57.670
weight.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.270
Is this awesome?

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:03.230
No. The model fits the training data too

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.030
well.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:07.950
In other words, we have low bias, but

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:11.510
probably very high variance.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.870
Gradient boost deals with this problem

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:15.950
by using a learning rate to scale the

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.510
contribution from the new tree.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.470
The learning rate is a value between

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:24.510
zero and one.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:26.550
In this case, we'll set the learning

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.790
rate to 0.1.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.790
Now, the predicted weight equals 71.2

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:37.470
+ 0.1 * 16.8,

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:41.310
which equals 72.9.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:44.270
With the learning rate set to 0.1,

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.150
the new prediction isn't as good as it

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.030
was before,

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:49.510
but it's a little better than the

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:51.430
prediction made with just the original

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.150
leaf, which predicted that all samples

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.270
would weigh 71.2.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.670
In other words, scaling the tree by the

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.230
learning rate results in a small step in

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.550
the right direction.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.270
According to the dude that invented

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:08.710
gradient boost, Jerome Friedman,

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.670
empirical evidence shows that taking

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.390
lots of small steps in the right

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:14.790
direction results in better predictions

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.670
with a testing data set, i.e. lower

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.670
variance.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.910
Bam!

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.990
So, let's build another tree so we can

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.030
take another small step in the right

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.030
direction.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:30.110
Just like before, we calculate the

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:32.630
pseudo residuals, the difference between

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:34.670
the observed weights and our latest

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:36.790
predictions.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.110
So, we plug in the observed weight

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:43.150
and the new predicted weight

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.710
and we get 15.1.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:48.630
And we save that in the column for

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:51.630
pseudo residuals.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:53.630
Then we repeat for all of the other

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:58.030
individuals in the training data set.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.630
Small bam.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:03.630
Note, these are the original residuals

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.430
from when our prediction was simply the

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:08.510
average overall weight.

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.670
And these are the residuals after adding

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.750
the new tree scaled by the learning

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:14.350
rate.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.550
The new residuals are all smaller than

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.350
before, so we've taken a small step in

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:21.350
the right direction.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:24.590
Double bam!

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.110
Now, let's build a new tree to predict

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.070
the new residuals.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:32.830
And here's the new tree.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.390
Note, in this simple example, the

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:37.470
branches are the same as before.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:40.230
However, in practice, the trees can be

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:43.070
different each time.

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.870
Just like before, since multiple samples

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:47.830
ended up in these leaves, we just

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:49.790
replace the residuals with their

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.830
averages.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.270
Now we combine the new tree with the

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:58.230
previous tree and the initial leaf.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.070
Note, we scale all of the trees by the

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:05.390
learning rate, which we set to 0.1.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:08.590
And add everything together.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.950
Now we're ready to make a new prediction

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:13.510
from the training data.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.670
Just like before, we start with the

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:17.790
initial prediction.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.910
Then add the scaled amount from the

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.630
first tree

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:23.630
and the scaled amount from the second

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:25.470
tree.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:29.150
That gives us 71.2

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:32.950
+ 0.1 * 16.8

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.710
+ 0.1 * 15.1

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:40.430
which equals 74.4.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:43.070
Which is another small step closer to

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.390
the observed weight.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:48.070
Now we use the initial leaf

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:50.350
plus the scaled values from the first

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:51.670
tree

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:53.870
plus the scaled values from the second

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:55.550
tree

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:59.230
to calculate new residuals.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:01.870
Remember, these were the residuals from

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:03.990
when we just used a single leaf to

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.150
predict weight.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.230
And these were the residuals after we

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.950
added the first tree to the prediction.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:13.990
And these are the residuals after we

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:17.510
added the second tree to the prediction.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.390
Each time we add a tree to the

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:23.190
prediction, the residuals get smaller.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.270
So we've taken another small step

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:28.670
towards making good predictions.

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:31.190
Now we build another tree to predict the

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.030
new residuals.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:36.230
And add it to the chain of trees that we

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.070
have already created.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:41.510
And we keep making trees until we reach

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.910
the maximum specified or adding

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:46.510
additional trees does not significantly

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:49.270
reduce the size of the residuals.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:50.870
Bam.

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:54.430
Then, when we get some new measurements,

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:56.750
we can predict weight

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:00.190
by starting with the initial prediction,

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:02.310
then adding the scaled value from the

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:04.310
first tree,

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.750
and the second tree,

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.310
and the third tree,

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:13.110
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.350
And once the math is all done, we are

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:18.790
left with the predicted weight.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:20.990
In this case, we predicted that this

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.790
person weighed 70 kg.

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.350
Triple bam.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:31.030
In summary, when gradient boost is used

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:33.150
for regression,

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.630
we start with a leaf that is the average

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.829
value of the variable we want to

00:14:37.839 --> 00:14:39.870
predict.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:42.270
In this case, we wanted to predict

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:44.350
weight.

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:46.230
Then we add a tree based on the

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:48.550
residuals, the difference between the

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:50.590
observed values and the predicted

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:52.910
values.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:54.990
And we scale the tree's contribution to

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.310
the final prediction with a learning

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:59.390
rate.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:01.590
Then we add another tree based on the

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.070
new residuals.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.150
And we keep adding trees based on the

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:09.590
errors made by the previous tree.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.510
That's all there is to it.

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:14.310
Bam.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:16.670
Tune in for part two in this series when

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:18.550
we dive deep into the math behind the

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:21.190
gradient boost algorithm for regression,

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:23.390
walking through it step by step, and

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:27.110
proving that it really is this simple.

